From c11d4e67ce355312adb6a4019ea2db03d4202ad3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "thang.luu" <thang.luu@titancorpvn.com>
Date: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 09:40:22 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(indicators): windowed rendering for streaming indicator
 series

lightweight-charts series.update() cost scales with the TOTAL point
count of the series, not the visible count - measured +6.5ms per live
tick with 9 plot series x 50k points, even though only ~300 bars were
on screen. Capping the rendered tail returned ticks to the
empty-chart floor.

This change renders only the most recent `sizeBars` points (default
5000) of each streaming-capable indicator plot series and backfills
older points automatically when the user pans toward the rendered
edge:

- pure window policy in src/indicators/renderWindow.ts (unit-tested):
  backfill triggers `guardBars` before the edge becomes visible (no
  visible gap), each extension reaches `expandBars` past the visible
  edge, and the window never shrinks during a session - a full-history
  pan simply converges to today's render-everything behavior.
- IndicatorController keeps the full computed points per plot as the
  backfill source, updated in place as bars close on the streaming
  path; extension re-applies the longer slice via setData.
- opt out or tune via
  `new IndicatorController(registry, { renderWindow })`.

Measured on the 50k-bar stress scenario (5 indicators, 30 ticks/s):
updateBar ~8.8ms -> ~2.9ms per tick; backfill verified by panning to
the first bar of the history with all indicator lines present.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
